IB/hfi1: fix PIO_CRED mmap using pointer arithmetic for a byte offset

Prepared against Linux v6.17 (e5f0a698b34ed76002dc5cff3804a61c80233a7a).

hfi1_file_mmap()'s PIO_CRED case computes cr_page_offset as a *byte*
offset (0, PAGE_SIZE or 2 * PAGE_SIZE) and then adds it to
dd->cr_base[].va, which is a `struct credit_return *`.  That is pointer
arithmetic, so the offset is scaled by sizeof(struct credit_return) == 64
and memvirt lands 64x too far - 256 KiB or 512 KiB past a 10240-byte
allocation.

memdma is unaffected: dma_addr_t is an integer type, so that line already
does byte arithmetic.

Consequences on a host whose user context has its credit-return entry on
the second or third credit-return page (hardware send context index >= 64,
which varies per boot):

  - dma_mmap_coherent() -> iommu_dma_mmap() is called with a cpu_addr that
    is inside the vmalloc range but belongs to no vm_area;
  - dma_common_find_pages() returns NULL, so the vm_map_pages() fast path
    is skipped;
  - vmalloc_to_pfn() -> vmalloc_to_page() returns NULL and page_to_pfn(NULL)
    yields (0 - vmemmap_base) / sizeof(struct page), a frame far above
    MAXPHYADDR;
  - remap_pfn_range() installs that frame, and the first user read of the
    credit-return page takes "Corrupted page table" / "Oops: Bad pagetable"
    with a reserved-bit fault.

Measured on a Dell T7610 (46-bit MAXPHYADDR, Intel IOMMU in DMA-FQ mode,
kernel 6.17.0-1030-oem) with kprobes on the mapping path:

  iommu_dma_mmap        cpu_addr=0xffffd3b88ef11000 dma_addr=0xffece000 size=0x1000
                        vmemmap_base=0xfffff71f00000000
  dma_common_find_pages ret=0x0
  vmalloc_to_pfn        ret=152538447872 (0x2384000000)
  remap_pfn_range       pfn=0x2384000000
  -> psm2_ep_open_pr: Corrupted page table at address ...
     PGD ... PTE 800049168e911235 / Oops: Bad pagetable: 000d

(0 - 0xfffff71f00000000) / 64 == 0x2384000000 exactly, confirming the
page_to_pfn(NULL) origin of the frame.

When the entry happens to be on the first page cr_page_offset is 0, the
scaling is a no-op and the mapping succeeds, which is why the failure is
intermittent across boots.

Introduced by commit 1ec82317a1da, which converted this case from
"virt_to_phys(va) + byte_offset" (integer arithmetic, correct) to the
memvirt/memdma form without casting away the pointer type.

--- a/drivers/infiniband/hw/hfi1/file_ops.c
+++ b/drivers/infiniband/hw/hfi1/file_ops.c
@@ -384,7 +384,7 @@ static int hfi1_file_mmap(struct file *fp, struct vm_area_struct *vma)
 		cr_page_offset = ((u64)uctxt->sc->hw_free -
 			  	     (u64)dd->cr_base[uctxt->numa_id].va) &
 				   PAGE_MASK;
-		memvirt = dd->cr_base[uctxt->numa_id].va + cr_page_offset;
+		memvirt = (u8 *)dd->cr_base[uctxt->numa_id].va + cr_page_offset;
 		memdma = dd->cr_base[uctxt->numa_id].dma + cr_page_offset;
 		memlen = PAGE_SIZE;
 		flags &= ~VM_MAYWRITE;
